## 1.2 Per-Game Folder Template

Every title is self-contained and follows an identical shape, so no game ever reaches into another:

```
src/games/<GameName>/
├── plan.md                  # Written in STEP 2, lives beside the code
├── index.js                 # Sole public export: default class <GameName> extends Game
├── config.js                # ALL tunables: physics constants, colors, bloom, difficulty curves
├── scene/                   # World construction (arena, lighting, environment, skybox)
├── entities/                # Gameplay actors (each Poolable where instanced)
├── systems/                 # Update systems (physics, spawn, scoring, AI, difficulty)
├── fx/                      # Game-specific VFX built ON TOP of shared/vfx
├── audio/                   # Game-specific synth recipes / music seed
├── ui/                      # Game-specific HUD extension + its scoped .css
└── dispose.js               # (only where teardown is nontrivial — usually handled by Disposer)
```

## 1.3 Import Scoping Law (enforced by ESLint rule + convention)

| From | May import |
|---|---|
| `shared/**` | `three`, other `shared/**`. **Never** `games/**`. |
| `games/X/**` | `three`, `../../shared/**`, siblings inside `games/X/**`. **Never** `games/Y/**`. |
| `hub/**` | `shared/**`, lazy `import()` of `games/*/index.js` only. |

Vite aliases kill deep `../../../` chains entirely:

```js
// vite.config.js
resolve: {
  alias: {
    '@shared': '/src/shared',
    '@game':   '/src/games',
    'three':   '/node_modules/three'   // single instance guarantee
  }
}
```

So every game imports as `@shared/vfx/VFXDirector.js` — flat, unambiguous, refactor-proof.

## 1.4 Vite Setup

```
package.json
  dependencies:    three
  devDependencies: vite
  scripts: dev / build / preview
```

`three` is the **only** runtime dependency. Post-processing comes from `three/examples/jsm/postprocessing/*` (EffectComposer, RenderPass, UnrealBloomPass, ShaderPass, SMAAPass, OutputPass) — first-party, not an external engine. No physics library, no tween library, no noise library (I write my own simplex), no audio library.

## 1.5 The Shared-Utilities Strategy — Contracts

**One renderer, many games.** `RendererFactory` owns a single `WebGLRenderer` for the whole session. Mounting a game hands it a fresh `Scene`/`Camera` and rebuilds only the `EffectComposer` passes; unmounting calls `Disposer.disposeAll()` on that game's ledger. This makes leak-testing trivial: after unmount, `renderer.info.memory.geometries` and `.textures` must return to the hub baseline. That assertion is wired into `DebugPanel`.

**One VFX façade.** Games never talk to `ParticleManager`, `CameraShake`, `HitStop`, and `Shockwave` individually. They call:

```js
VFX.impact({ position, normal, power, color, hitStop: 0.06, shockwave: true });
```

`VFXDirector` translates `power` (normalized impact energy) into trauma, particle count, hit-stop duration, and shockwave radius using one shared curve — which is why every game will *feel* like the same premium product.

**One particle budget.** `ParticleManager` is a hard 500-instance ceiling backed by `InstancedMesh`. Every emit request carries a `priority`. At saturation, incoming high-priority particles evict the oldest lowest-priority live particles rather than being dropped — so a boss explosion always reads, even mid-firefight. Count is exposed for the adaptive quality governor.

**One base class.** `Game` defines the lifecycle every title implements: `init(ctx)`, `fixedUpdate(dt)`, `update(dt, alpha)`, `render()`, `onResize(w,h)`, `onPause()`, `dispose()`. `Loop` drives a fixed 120 Hz simulation step with an accumulator and renders with interpolation `alpha`, so collision math is frame-rate independent on every machine — mandatory for the swept-AABB in Breakout and the jump derivatives in Donkey Kong.

